      "version": 27,
      "versionNonce": 814100282,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787191328894,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Step 2 — High-Level Design: narrate a simple end-to-end flow first (client → API → DB), then layer in components only as the stated requirements demand — don't open with a diagram full of boxes you can't justify.\n- API Design: define the contract before the internals — endpoint/RPC signatures, request/response shapes, idempotency keys on writes, pagination strategy. State REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL with a one-line reason, not a lecture.\n- Step 3 — Deep Dive: let the interviewer steer. Offer 2-3 candidate topics ('I could go deeper on data model, consistency, or the write path — which is most useful?') rather than picking unilaterally.\n- Deep Dive: every tradeoff needs a because — 'I'd pick eventual consistency because read-after-write isn't a hard requirement here' beats naming CAP theorem in isolation.\n- Step 4 — Wrap Up: proactively name what you'd cut for time — bottlenecks, single points of failure, monitoring/alerting, cost. Signals you know the design is time-boxed, not 'done.'\n- Wrap Up: summarize in under 60 seconds — requirements satisfied, one open risk, one next step you'd take with more time.\n- Anti-pattern: designing for infinite scale on day one. Size the system to the actual load estimated in step 1, and state the explicit trigger for the next scaling step.\n- Failure-mode reasoning: for every dependency added, ask 'what happens when this is slow, down, or returns garbage?' — an un-timed-out downstream call is the most common cascading-failure bug, in interviews and in prod.\n- Anti-pattern: synchronous chains of more than 2-3 services per user request. Prefer async/queue-based decoupling once a call chain risks compounding p99 latency (multiply per-hop p99s, don't just add them).\n- Split-brain and dual-writes: never assume two systems (cache + DB, or two DBs) stay in sync without an explicit reconciliation or write-ordering strategy — call this out even if you don't solve it live.\n- Retries amplify load under failure ('retry storms'). Always pair retries with exponential backoff + jitter, a max-attempt ceiling, and idempotent operations downstream.\n- Senior vs. staff: senior gets the design correct; staff also quantifies cost/complexity — 'this saves 40ms p99 but adds on-call burden and $X/month for marginal benefit at our scale' — said unprompted.\n- Staff-level signal: naming unknown-unknowns explicitly — 'I'm assuming writes are read-heavy 10:1; if that's wrong the caching strategy changes' — instead of presenting assumptions as facts.\n- Staff-level signal: propose the boring, operationally-proven solution first (managed Postgres before a bespoke sharded KV store), and only reach for novelty when a specific requirement demands it.\n- Senior vs. staff on ambiguity: senior clarifies once at the start; staff keeps re-scoping throughout, flagging when an earlier answer would change if a stated assumption turns out false.\n- Practice pacing against a clock (5/20/15/5). Running out of time mid deep-dive is the single most common way a technically strong candidate is scored down.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Step 2 — High-Level Design: narrate a simple end-to-end flow first (client → API → DB), then layer in components only as the stated requirements demand — don't open with a diagram full of boxes you can't justify.\n- API Design: define the contract before the internals — endpoint/RPC signatures, request/response shapes, idempotency keys on writes, pagination strategy. State REST vs gRPC vs GraphQL with a one-line reason, not a lecture.\n- Step 3 — Deep Dive: let the interviewer steer. Offer 2-3 candidate topics ('I could go deeper on data model, consistency, or the write path — which is most useful?') rather than picking unilaterally.\n- Deep Dive: every tradeoff needs a because — 'I'd pick eventual consistency because read-after-write isn't a hard requirement here' beats naming CAP theorem in isolation.\n- Step 4 — Wrap Up: proactively name what you'd cut for time — bottlenecks, single points of failure, monitoring/alerting, cost. Signals you know the design is time-boxed, not 'done.'\n- Wrap Up: summarize in under 60 seconds — requirements satisfied, one open risk, one next step you'd take with more time.\n- Anti-pattern: designing for infinite scale on day one. Size the system to the actual load estimated in step 1, and state the explicit trigger for the next scaling step.\n- Failure-mode reasoning: for every dependency added, ask 'what happens when this is slow, down, or returns garbage?' — an un-timed-out downstream call is the most common cascading-failure bug, in interviews and in prod.\n- Anti-pattern: synchronous chains of more than 2-3 services per user request. Prefer async/queue-based decoupling once a call chain risks compounding p99 latency (multiply per-hop p99s, don't just add them).\n- Split-brain and dual-writes: never assume two systems (cache + DB, or two DBs) stay in sync without an explicit reconciliation or write-ordering strategy — call this out even if you don't solve it live.\n- Retries amplify load under failure ('retry storms'). Always pair retries with exponential backoff + jitter, a max-attempt ceiling, and idempotent operations downstream.\n- Senior vs. staff: senior gets the design correct; staff also quantifies cost/complexity — 'this saves 40ms p99 but adds on-call burden and $X/month for marginal benefit at our scale' — said unprompted.\n- Staff-level signal: naming unknown-unknowns explicitly — 'I'm assuming writes are read-heavy 10:1; if that's wrong the caching strategy changes' — instead of presenting assumptions as facts.\n- Staff-level signal: propose the boring, operationally-proven solution first (managed Postgres before a bespoke sharded KV store), and only reach for novelty when a specific requirement demands it.\n- Senior vs. staff on ambiguity: senior clarifies once at the start; staff keeps re-scoping throughout, flagging when an earlier answer would change if a stated assumption turns out false.\n- Practice pacing against a clock (5/20/15/5). Running out of time mid deep-dive is the single most common way a technically strong candidate is scored down.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "b1Q"
